= Working in This Industry After Graduation

The honest answer to the guideline's final question — would I consider
working in this industry after graduation? — is that I have already
signed the contract.

I accepted a full-time return offer from Google before the close of the
internship. There were three reasons. The first is the depth of the work
itself. ETM in Perfetto was an unusually challenging intern project, and
the version of the role I have signed for opens onto a similar problem
space with more responsibility and more scope. The second is the team:
the people I worked with, both on Web Performance and across the
adjacent teams I collaborated with, set a technical standard that I want
to keep being measured against. The third is the *style* of work — the
combination of rigorous code review, written design culture, and the
expectation that the underlying mathematical structure of a problem is
allowed to drive the engineering — which is the style I want to spend
the early part of my career inside.

That said, I do not consider this an end-point. Working in industry
right out of an undergraduate degree is one valid trajectory for a
mathematics graduate, but it is not the only one, and I expect to keep
re-evaluating it. For now: yes. For the medium term: I will know more
when I have spent longer in the role.
